@prefix ldh:    <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix dh:     <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub/document-hierarchy#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<> a dh:Item ;
    dct:title "Assistant" ;
    dct:description "The built-in assistant: how questions become Web-Algebra plans, how it is set up, and how conversations are stored" ;
    rdf:_1 <#content> .

<#content> a ldh:XHTML ;
    rdf:value """<div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <p class="ldh-body-lg">The built-in assistant: how questions become Web-Algebra plans, how it is set up, and how conversations are stored</p>
    <p>The assistant turns a question into a <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/Web-Algebra" target="_blank">Web-Algebra</a> plan, shows the plan, and runs
        it when the reader says so. It runs in the browser as part of the client stylesheet and talks to a <samp>web-algebra</samp> service beside the platform.
        The <a href="../../user-guide/use-the-assistant/">user guide</a> covers using it. To work with LinkedDataHub from an agent of your own instead, see
        <a href="../../get-started/agents/">Connect an agent</a>.</p>
    <div>
        <h2 id="architecture">How it works</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>The client sends the question to <samp>/webalgebra/plans</samp> on the dataspace's own origin, with the current document, the dataspace's
                SPARQL endpoint and ontology, and the last three turns of the conversation that ran, each with its plan, its outcome and up to a hundred
                of the rows it returned.</li>
            <li>nginx forwards <samp>/webalgebra</samp> to the <samp>web-algebra</samp> service together with the client certificate of the connection. A
                request without a client certificate is answered with <samp>401</samp>, since the service has nobody to act for.</li>
            <li>The service asks the model for a plan, checks it against its executor, and answers with the plan, a one-sentence summary and the operations
                it uses. The client shows them as a card.</li>
            <li>On Execute, the client submits the plan to <samp>/webalgebra</samp>. The service answers <samp>202</samp> with an execution id, and the
                client polls <samp>/webalgebra/{id}/result</samp> once a second, showing each step as the executor reports it.</li>
            <li>When the plan has run, the client sends the result to <samp>/webalgebra/answers</samp> for a sentence that sums it up, then
                <a href="#data-model">stores the turn</a> in the conversation's document and reloads the documents the plan wrote.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>A plan that only reads may run as soon as it arrives. One that writes always waits for the reader to press Execute.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="access-control">Access control</h2>
        <p>The service runs every plan as the reader who asked. It presents the secretary's WebID certificate to the platform, with an
            <code>On-Behalf-Of</code> header naming the reader's WebID. The platform honours it because every agent's WebID document that the platform creates,
            at <a href="../../get-started/get-an-account/">sign-up</a> or at the first Google or ORCID login, states that the secretary delegates that agent
            (<code>&lt;secretary&gt; acl:delegates &lt;agent&gt;</code>). Plans are therefore subject to the reader's own
            <a href="../administration/acl/">authorizations</a>: a plan can read and write what the reader can, and nothing else.</p>
        <p>nginx clears any <code>On-Behalf-Of</code> and <code>Client-Cert</code> headers arriving from outside, so identity comes only from the TLS
            connection. The <samp>web-algebra</samp> service is reachable only through nginx.</p>
        <p>The <span class="ac-btn in-neutral ap-outline sz-md">Assistant</span> button appears for a signed-in agent with append access to the document,
            and a conversation accepts new questions under the same condition.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
        <p><samp>docker-compose.yml</samp> includes the <samp>web-algebra</samp> service and the nginx route to it, so the assistant can run plans out of the
            box. Writing plans from questions needs an <a href="https://platform.openai.com/api-keys" target="_blank">OpenAI API key</a>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Put the key in <samp>secrets/openai_api_key.txt</samp></li>
            <li>Declare the secret and hand it to the service in <samp>docker-compose.override.yml</samp>:
                <pre>secrets:
  openai_api_key:
    file: ./secrets/openai_api_key.txt
services:
  web-algebra:
    environment:
      - OPENAI_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/openai_api_key
    secrets:
      - secretary_cert_password
      - openai_api_key</pre>
            </li>
            <li>Recreate the service: <samp>docker compose up -d web-algebra</samp></li>
        </ol>
        <p>Without a key the service still runs plans, but declines to write them: a question is answered with a message saying so, shown in the card.
            The sentence that sums up a result also comes from the model; without it, the card shows the rows alone.</p>
        <p>The service's variables are listed in the <a href="../configuration/#web-algebra">configuration reference</a>.</p>
        <div>
            <h3 id="without-service">Running without the service</h3>
            <p>A deployment that does not want the assistant can leave the <samp>web-algebra</samp> service out, for example with a Compose
                <a href="https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/profiles/" target="_blank">profile</a>. nginx's <samp>depends_on</samp> and its
                <samp>/webalgebra</samp> location refer to the service, so both need adjusting too. The Assistant button does not check whether the service
                exists, so on such a deployment a question fails with an error in its card.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="data-model">Data model</h2>
        <p>A conversation is an <code>ldh:Chat</code> resource, placed in a document by an <a href="../data-model/blocks/#object-blocks">object block</a>
            like a view or a chart. Its turns are <code>ldh:ChatTurn</code> resources in the same document, and the chat's <code>rdf:_1</code>,
            <code>rdf:_2</code>, … members in the order they were asked. The first question writes the chat, the object block and the document's next
            <code>rdf:_N</code> for the block in one request; the chat and its block share a fragment suffix, and the chat's title is the first question, cut near
            80 characters. Each turn is written when its answer has landed, or has failed to.</p>
        <table>
            <caption class="ac-vh">Properties of an assistant turn</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Property</th>
                    <th scope="col">Value</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>ldh:question</code></th>
                    <td>What the reader asked</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>ldh:answer</code></th>
                    <td>The sentence summing up what the plan did, when the model gave one</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>ldh:plan</code></th>
                    <td>The Web-Algebra plan as an <code>rdf:XMLLiteral</code>: its summary, its operations and the plan itself</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>ldh:execution</code></th>
                    <td>What running the plan reported, as an <code>rdf:XMLLiteral</code>: the steps, the documents written, and the first hundred rows or resources of the result</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>ldh:outcome</code></th>
                    <td>How the turn went, in one line: rows or resources returned, documents written, no results, the failure's message, or <samp>declined</samp> when the service answered in words instead of with a plan</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>dct:created</code></th>
                    <td>When the turn was stored</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>A stored conversation is drawn from these resources when the document is rendered again, so it shows the result as it was when the turn ran;
            nothing is run again. Since the chat is an ordinary resource, its object block can also be placed in another document, and questions asked
            there are stored in the chat's own document.</p>
        <pre>@prefix ldh: &lt;https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#&gt; .
@prefix rdf: &lt;http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#&gt; .
@prefix dct: &lt;http://purl.org/dc/terms/&gt; .
@prefix xsd: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#&gt; .

&lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/&gt; rdf:_3 &lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/#block-91c0…&gt; .

&lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/#block-91c0…&gt; a ldh:Object ;
    rdf:value &lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/#chat-91c0…&gt; .

&lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/#chat-91c0…&gt; a ldh:Chat ;
    dct:title "Which five customers ordered the most in 1997?" ;
    rdf:_1 &lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/#turn-5d7e…&gt; .

&lt;https://localhost:4443/customers/#turn-5d7e…&gt; a ldh:ChatTurn ;
    ldh:question "Which five customers ordered the most in 1997?" ;
    ldh:plan "&lt;wa:plan …&gt;…&lt;/wa:plan&gt;"^^rdf:XMLLiteral ;
    ldh:execution "&lt;wa:execution …&gt;…&lt;/wa:execution&gt;"^^rdf:XMLLiteral ;
    ldh:outcome "5 rows" ;
    dct:created "2026-10-17T09:12:44Z"^^xsd:dateTime .</pre>
    </div>
</div>"""^^rdf:XMLLiteral .
